-- ID 721: State/Local Government — Modified accrual vs full accrual revenue comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'The City of Ashford levies $3 million in property taxes on January 1. By fiscal year-end (June 30), $2.6 million has been collected. An additional $250,000 is collected in July and August (within 60 days), and the remaining $150,000 is expected to be collected in October. How would the $3 million levy be recognized differently under modified accrual (fund-level statements) versus full accrual (government-wide statements), and why do the amounts differ?',
  choices = '["Fund-level recognizes $3 million and government-wide recognizes $2.6 million, because fund statements use the full levy while government-wide statements use only cash collected", "Both recognize $3 million because the full levy is measurable and the collection timing is irrelevant under both bases", "Fund-level recognizes $2.85 million (cash collected plus amounts available within 60 days) while government-wide recognizes $3 million (full levy less estimated uncollectibles) — the difference arises because modified accrual adds an availability criterion that full accrual does not require, causing the $150,000 collected beyond 60 days to be deferred at the fund level", "Fund-level recognizes $2.6 million (cash basis) while government-wide recognizes $2.85 million, because government-wide includes the 60-day availability window"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under modified accrual (GASB fund-level reporting), revenue must be both measurable and available. Available means collected within the current period or soon enough thereafter to pay current liabilities — typically 60 days per GASB Statement No. 33. The $2.6M collected by June 30 plus $250,000 collected within 60 days totals $2.85M recognized as revenue; the $150,000 expected in October is deferred as a deferred inflow of resources. Under full accrual (government-wide statements per GASB 34), revenue is recognized when the enforceable legal claim arises — at the levy date — so the full $3M (less any estimated uncollectibles) is recognized regardless of collection timing. The $150,000 difference illustrates how the availability criterion in modified accrual creates a more conservative, cash-proximate measure of revenue. (A) reverses the relationship — fund-level is more restrictive, not less. (B) is wrong because modified accrual explicitly requires availability, not just measurability. (D) is wrong because the 60-day window applies to fund-level modified accrual, not to government-wide full accrual.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 721;

-- ID 724: State/Local Government — Capital asset dual reporting analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'The City of Brookfield purchases a fire truck for $450,000 from its General Fund. How is this transaction reported differently in the governmental fund financial statements versus the government-wide financial statements, and why does GASB require this dual treatment?',
  choices = '["Both statements report a $450,000 capital asset and depreciate it over its useful life, because GASB 34 eliminated differences between fund-level and government-wide treatment of capital assets", "The governmental fund statements report a $450,000 expenditure that reduces fund balance in the current period, while the government-wide statements capitalize the $450,000 as an asset and depreciate it over its useful life — this dual treatment exists because fund statements focus on current financial resources and spending accountability, while government-wide statements focus on long-term economic resources and operational cost measurement", "The governmental fund statements capitalize the asset while the government-wide statements expense it, because government-wide statements use a shorter-term measurement focus", "Both statements report a $450,000 expenditure because governments cannot capitalize assets under GASB standards"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under GASB 34, governmental fund statements use the current financial resources measurement focus with modified accrual accounting. Capital asset purchases consume current financial resources, so the $450,000 is reported as an expenditure — reducing fund balance immediately. The government-wide statements use the economic resources measurement focus with full accrual accounting, so the $450,000 is capitalized as a capital asset and depreciated over its useful life. This dual treatment serves different accountability objectives: fund statements show whether the government lived within its current-period budget, while government-wide statements show the cost of services delivered over time. (A) is wrong because GASB 34 specifically preserved different treatments at the fund level vs. government-wide level. (C) reverses the treatment — fund statements expense, government-wide capitalize. (D) is wrong because GASB 34 requires capitalization of capital assets in government-wide statements.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 724;

-- ID 729: State/Local Government — 60-day availability criterion analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'The City of Dunmore levies $5 million in property taxes. By December 31 (fiscal year-end), $4.2 million has been collected. During January, $500,000 is collected. During February, $200,000 is collected. The remaining $100,000 is collected in April. The city uses a 60-day availability period. How does the 60-day availability criterion affect revenue recognition at the fund level, and what happens to the amounts collected after the cutoff?',
  choices = '["Revenue is $5 million because the full levy is recognized regardless of collection timing once the legal claim is established", "Revenue is $4.2 million because only cash collected by year-end qualifies — the 60-day rule applies only to expenditures, not revenue", "Revenue is $4.9 million ($4.2M collected by year-end plus $500,000 in January and $200,000 in February, all within 60 days) — the $100,000 collected in April is recorded as a deferred inflow of resources at December 31 and recognized as revenue in the following fiscal year when it becomes available", "Revenue is $4.7 million because only the January collections qualify under the 60-day rule, and February and April collections are both excluded"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under modified accrual accounting (GASB Statement No. 33), property tax revenue is recognized when measurable and available. Available means collected within the period or soon enough thereafter — typically within 60 days of year-end. The $4.2M collected by December 31, the $500,000 collected in January (within 60 days), and the $200,000 collected in February (within 60 days) all meet the availability criterion, totaling $4.9M. The $100,000 collected in April exceeds the 60-day window and is reported as a deferred inflow of resources on the December 31 balance sheet — it will be recognized as revenue in the next fiscal year when those resources become available. (A) is wrong because modified accrual requires the availability criterion, not just the enforceable claim. (B) is wrong because the 60-day rule explicitly applies to revenue recognition under modified accrual. (D) is wrong because the 60-day window extends through February (60 days from December 31 reaches approximately March 1), so February collections qualify.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 729;

-- ID 736: State/Local Government — Bond premium dual reporting analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'The City of Ferndale issues $10 million of general obligation bonds at a premium of $400,000. How is the $400,000 premium reported differently in the governmental fund statements versus the government-wide statements, and why does the treatment differ?',
  choices = '["The governmental fund statements report the $400,000 premium as an other financing source that increases fund balance in the current period, while the government-wide statements report it as part of the total bond liability ($10.4 million) that is amortized over the life of the bonds — the difference arises because fund statements treat inflows of financial resources as current-period sources, while government-wide statements reflect the long-term economic obligation", "Both statements report the premium as revenue because it represents cash received in excess of the bond''s face value", "The governmental fund statements report the premium as a long-term liability while government-wide statements expense it immediately", "Both statements amortize the premium over the bond''s life because GASB 34 requires identical treatment of bond premiums at all reporting levels"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under the current financial resources measurement focus, governmental fund statements recognize the $400,000 premium as an other financing source — a current-period inflow that increases fund balance. The fund statements do not report the long-term liability itself (only the current portion due). Under the economic resources measurement focus in government-wide statements (GASB 34), the full $10.4 million ($10M face + $400K premium) is reported as a long-term liability, and the premium is amortized as a reduction of interest expense over the bonds'' life. This dual treatment reflects different purposes: fund statements measure whether current resources are sufficient for current obligations, while government-wide statements measure total long-term obligations and the true cost of borrowing. (B) is wrong because bond premiums are other financing sources, not revenue, in governmental accounting. (C) reverses the treatment — fund statements do not report long-term liabilities. (D) is wrong because GASB 34 specifically requires different treatments at the two reporting levels.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 736;

-- ID 738: State/Local Government — Measurement focus impact on transaction reporting
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A city government purchases a $200,000 vehicle using General Fund resources and simultaneously makes a $150,000 principal payment on outstanding bonds. Analyze how the difference in measurement focus — current financial resources (fund-level) versus economic resources (government-wide) — causes these two transactions to be reported differently in each set of financial statements.',
  choices = '["Both transactions are reported identically in both statements because GASB 34 harmonized the measurement focuses for capital assets and debt", "Under current financial resources, both are expenditures totaling $350,000 that reduce fund balance; under economic resources, the vehicle is capitalized as a $200,000 asset and the debt payment reduces the bond liability by $150,000 with no expense — the measurement focus determines whether outflows consume current resources (fund) or transform into assets and reduce liabilities (government-wide)", "Under current financial resources, the vehicle is capitalized and the debt payment is expensed; under economic resources, both are expensed — fund statements preserve asset values while government-wide statements focus on period costs", "Both transactions reduce cash equally in both statements, and the measurement focus only affects the classification labels, not the amounts reported"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The current financial resources measurement focus (governmental fund statements) asks: did we spend current financial resources? Both the $200,000 vehicle purchase and $150,000 debt payment consume current resources, so both are reported as expenditures that reduce fund balance. The economic resources measurement focus (government-wide statements) asks: what happened to all economic resources? The vehicle purchase creates a long-lived capital asset ($200,000), so it is capitalized and depreciated — no current-period expense for the full amount. The debt payment reduces the outstanding bond liability by $150,000 — this is a balance sheet transaction, not an expense. This illustrates GASB 34''s core design: fund statements show how resources were spent, while government-wide statements show how the government''s overall financial position changed. (A) is wrong because GASB 34 intentionally maintains these differences. (C) reverses the treatment entirely. (D) is wrong because the measurement focus changes both the amounts and the statement classifications, not just labels.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 738;

-- ID 3225: State/Local Government — Deferred inflows from property tax timing
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A county government with a December 31 fiscal year-end levies $8 million in property taxes. By year-end, $6.5 million is collected and $1 million is collected in January (within the 60-day availability period). The remaining $500,000 is expected in March through June. The county records $500,000 as a deferred inflow of resources. Analyze how this deferred inflow is created under modified accrual and what happens when the $500,000 becomes available in the following fiscal year.',
  choices = '["The deferred inflow is reversed in the next year and recognized as prior-period revenue through a restatement, because the tax was levied in the prior year", "The $500,000 deferred inflow is never recognized as revenue — it remains permanently deferred because the availability period has passed", "The $500,000 deferred inflow is created because the amount was levied (measurable) but not collected within the availability period (not available) — in the following fiscal year, as the cash is collected, the deferred inflow is reduced and revenue is recognized, because the resources have now become available to finance expenditures of that period", "The deferred inflow is an estimate of uncollectible taxes and is written off as bad debt expense in the next fiscal year"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under GASB''s modified accrual framework, property tax revenue requires both measurability and availability. The $500,000 meets the measurability criterion (the levy establishes the amount) but fails the availability criterion (not collected within 60 days of year-end). This creates a deferred inflow of resources — an item that consumes net position of a future period. When the cash is collected in March through June of the following fiscal year, the deferred inflow is reduced and revenue is recognized in that period because the resources are now available. This is not a restatement — the revenue is intentionally recognized in the period when the resources become available to pay obligations. (A) is wrong because this is prospective recognition, not a prior-period restatement. (B) is wrong because the deferred inflow is temporary and converts to revenue upon collection. (D) is wrong because deferred inflows are not loss provisions — the taxes are expected to be collected, just not within the availability window.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 3225;

-- ID 3233: State/Local Government — Net position component analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A city government acquires a $5 million building. In Scenario A, the city finances the purchase with a $5 million general obligation bond. In Scenario B, the city pays cash from accumulated General Fund reserves. Analyze how the financing method affects the "net investment in capital assets" and "unrestricted" components of net position in the government-wide statements, and explain why total net position differs between the scenarios.',
  choices = '["In Scenario A, net investment in capital assets is $0 ($5M asset minus $5M debt) and unrestricted net position is unchanged; in Scenario B, net investment in capital assets is $5M and unrestricted decreases by $5M — total net position is the same in both scenarios because the financing method only shifts amounts between components without changing the total", "In Scenario A, net investment in capital assets is $5M and unrestricted is unchanged because the debt is reported separately; in Scenario B, net investment in capital assets is $0 because cash-funded assets are not capitalized", "In both scenarios, net investment in capital assets is $5M because the financing source does not affect capitalization, and unrestricted is unchanged", "In Scenario A, net investment in capital assets is $0 ($5M asset minus $5M related debt) and unrestricted net position is unchanged because no existing resources were consumed; in Scenario B, net investment in capital assets is $5M and unrestricted decreases by $5M because existing resources funded the purchase — however, total net position is lower in Scenario A due to the new liability, which reduces overall net position"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): The "net investment in capital assets" component equals capital assets minus related outstanding debt minus unspent bond proceeds. In Scenario A (debt-financed), this component is $5M - $5M = $0, and unrestricted net position is unchanged because no existing resources were used. However, total net position decreases because the $5M liability is added without a corresponding increase in non-capital resources. In Scenario B (cash), net investment in capital assets is $5M (no related debt), and unrestricted decreases by $5M (cash depleted). Total net position is unchanged because one asset (cash) was exchanged for another (building) with no new liability. The key analytical insight under GASB 34 is that debt financing shifts total net position downward while cash financing merely reclassifies existing net position among components. (A) is wrong because total net position is not the same — Scenario A adds a liability that Scenario B does not. (B) is wrong because cash-funded assets are capitalized. (C) is wrong because the financing source directly affects the net investment calculation.',
  correct_index = 3
WHERE id = 3233;

-- ============================================================
-- DERIVATIVES AND HEDGING (6 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 706: Derivatives — Hedge accounting vs no hedge accounting income volatility
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Oakmont Industries holds a derivative that gains $75,000 in fair value during the quarter. In Scenario A, the derivative qualifies for cash flow hedge accounting under ASC 815. In Scenario B, the identical derivative does not qualify for hedge accounting. How does the $75,000 gain affect income statement volatility differently in each scenario, and why does hedge accounting produce this effect?',
  choices = '["In both scenarios the $75,000 gain is reported in net income immediately, because ASC 815 requires all derivative gains to flow through the income statement regardless of hedge designation", "In Scenario A, the $75,000 gain is never recognized anywhere because hedge accounting eliminates all derivative gains and losses; in Scenario B, the gain is recognized in equity", "In both scenarios the $75,000 gain is reported in OCI because all derivative gains bypass the income statement under ASC 815", "In Scenario A (hedge accounting), the $75,000 gain is reported in other comprehensive income (OCI) and reclassified to earnings when the hedged forecasted transaction occurs, reducing income volatility; in Scenario B (no hedge), the full $75,000 gain hits net income immediately — hedge accounting reduces earnings volatility by deferring recognition until the gain can offset the loss on the hedged item"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): Under ASC 815, a cash flow hedge''s effective gains and losses are initially recorded in other comprehensive income (OCI), not net income. The $75,000 gain stays in accumulated OCI until the hedged forecasted transaction (e.g., a future purchase or sale) affects earnings, at which point the gain is reclassified from OCI to net income to offset the hedged item''s impact. Without hedge accounting (Scenario B), the same $75,000 gain flows directly to net income in the current period — creating volatility because the derivative gain is recognized without the offsetting effect of the hedged transaction. This timing mismatch is exactly what hedge accounting is designed to solve. (A) is wrong because the entire purpose of cash flow hedge accounting is to defer income statement recognition. (B) is wrong because hedge accounting does not eliminate gains — it defers them in OCI. (C) is wrong because undesignated derivatives must be marked to fair value through earnings, not OCI.',
  correct_index = 3
WHERE id = 706;

-- ID 707: Derivatives — Fair value hedge vs cash flow hedge income statement comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Westbrook Corp. designates a derivative as a fair value hedge on inventory. Crestone Corp. designates an identical derivative as a cash flow hedge on a forecasted purchase. Both derivatives gain $50,000 in value during the period. Compare the income statement effects of the $50,000 gain for each company in the current period, and analyze why the difference matters for earnings volatility.',
  choices = '["Westbrook (fair value hedge) recognizes the $50,000 derivative gain in earnings and simultaneously adjusts the hedged inventory''s carrying amount by the $50,000 loss, producing minimal net earnings impact; Crestone (cash flow hedge) defers the $50,000 gain in OCI with no current earnings effect — fair value hedges offset within current earnings while cash flow hedges defer recognition entirely, both reducing volatility through different mechanisms", "Both companies recognize $50,000 in net income because ASC 815 treats all hedges identically for income statement purposes", "Westbrook defers the gain in OCI while Crestone recognizes it in earnings, because fair value hedges protect future transactions while cash flow hedges protect existing assets", "Neither company recognizes any amount because the hedges are perfectly effective and ASC 815 eliminates all gains and losses on effective hedges"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under ASC 815, a fair value hedge produces an earnings offset in the current period: Westbrook recognizes the $50,000 derivative gain in earnings but also adjusts the hedged inventory''s carrying amount for the offsetting $50,000 loss (the change in fair value of the hedged risk), resulting in minimal net income impact. A cash flow hedge defers the derivative''s effective gain in OCI — Crestone reports $0 in current earnings from the hedge, with the $50,000 reclassified from OCI when the forecasted purchase affects earnings. Both approaches reduce volatility: fair value hedges through concurrent offset in earnings, cash flow hedges through deferral in OCI. This distinction matters because fair value hedges still show gross gains and losses in the income statement (they offset), while cash flow hedges keep earnings completely clean until the hedged transaction occurs. (B) is wrong because the two hedge types use fundamentally different recognition mechanisms. (C) reverses the treatment. (D) is wrong because effectiveness does not eliminate recognition — it determines where gains/losses are reported.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 707;

-- ID 710: Derivatives — Interest rate swap risk profile analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Harlow Manufacturing has $20 million in fixed-rate debt at 5.5%. The CFO enters a receive-fixed, pay-floating interest rate swap with a notional amount of $20 million. The floating rate is SOFR plus 1.2%. Analyze how this swap changes Harlow''s interest rate risk profile, and explain why the CFO might choose this strategy during a period when interest rates are expected to decline.',
  choices = '["The swap converts Harlow from fixed-rate to effectively floating-rate exposure — the company pays 5.5% fixed on the debt but receives 5.5% fixed on the swap and pays SOFR + 1.2% on the swap, creating a net floating-rate position; if rates decline as expected, SOFR + 1.2% drops below 5.5%, reducing Harlow''s total effective interest cost below what it would pay on the fixed debt alone", "The swap eliminates all interest rate risk because the fixed payments on the debt and swap cancel out, leaving Harlow with zero net interest expense", "The swap increases Harlow''s fixed-rate exposure by layering additional fixed payments on top of the existing debt, doubling the company''s interest cost", "The swap has no effect on Harlow''s risk profile because receive-fixed swaps are only used for speculative purposes and do not interact with existing debt positions"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): By entering a receive-fixed, pay-floating swap on a notional equal to its fixed-rate debt, Harlow synthetically converts from a fixed-rate borrower to a floating-rate borrower. The economics: Harlow pays 5.5% on the debt, receives approximately 5.5% on the swap (offsetting the debt payment), and pays SOFR + 1.2% on the swap. The net position is effectively SOFR + 1.2%. If rates decline, SOFR decreases and Harlow''s total cost falls below 5.5% — capturing the benefit of lower rates. This is a deliberate bet on the direction of interest rates under ASC 815, and it can qualify for fair value hedge accounting if properly documented. (B) is wrong because the swap replaces fixed exposure with floating exposure — it changes the type of risk rather than eliminating it. (C) is wrong because the receive-fixed leg offsets the existing fixed obligation; it does not add to it. (D) is wrong because receive-fixed swaps are commonly used to manage existing debt portfolios, not solely for speculation.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 710;

-- ID 3188: Derivatives — Hedge effectiveness failure mid-period analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Kensington Corp. designated a derivative as a cash flow hedge that has been effective for two years. At the end of Q2, the hedge fails the effectiveness test under ASC 815. At that point, $120,000 of gains are accumulated in OCI from prior periods. Analyze the effect of the hedge disqualification on Kensington''s balance sheet and income statement, and explain what happens to the $120,000 already in OCI.',
  choices = '["The $120,000 in OCI is immediately reclassified to net income as a cumulative catch-up adjustment, and all future derivative gains and losses are recognized in earnings because the hedge designation is terminated", "The $120,000 in OCI is reversed against retained earnings as an error correction because the hedge should never have been designated", "The $120,000 in OCI remains in accumulated OCI and is reclassified to earnings when the forecasted transaction affects earnings (or is recognized immediately if the forecasted transaction is no longer probable) — going forward, the derivative remains on the balance sheet at fair value but all subsequent changes in fair value are reported directly in earnings because hedge accounting no longer applies", "The derivative is removed from the balance sheet entirely when it fails the effectiveness test, and the $120,000 in OCI is written off as a loss"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under ASC 815, when a cash flow hedge fails the effectiveness test, the previously accumulated gains or losses in OCI are not immediately dumped into earnings. The $120,000 remains in accumulated OCI and follows the normal reclassification pattern — it is reclassified to earnings in the period(s) when the hedged forecasted transaction affects earnings. However, if the forecasted transaction is no longer probable, the amount in OCI must be reclassified to earnings immediately. Prospectively, the derivative is no longer designated as a hedge, so it remains on the balance sheet at fair value with all subsequent fair value changes recognized in earnings. This creates a bifurcated treatment: legacy OCI follows the hedged transaction, while new changes hit earnings. (A) is wrong because the $120,000 is not immediately reclassified unless the forecasted transaction is no longer probable. (B) is wrong because a hedge that was previously effective is not an error — the disqualification is prospective. (D) is wrong because derivatives are always carried at fair value on the balance sheet regardless of hedge designation.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 3188;

-- ID 3195: Derivatives — Fair value hedge offset vs undesignated derivative volatility
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ashford Trading holds $2 million of commodity inventory. In Scenario A, Ashford designates a derivative as a fair value hedge under ASC 815 — the derivative gains $85,000 while the hedged inventory loses $80,000 in fair value. In Scenario B, Ashford holds an identical undesignated derivative that gains $85,000 with no hedge accounting applied. Compare the net income effects in each scenario and analyze how the offsetting adjustments under fair value hedge accounting reduce earnings volatility.',
  choices = '["In Scenario A, net income includes a $5,000 net gain (derivative gain of $85,000 minus inventory write-down of $80,000) because fair value hedge accounting adjusts both the derivative and the hedged item through earnings simultaneously; in Scenario B, net income includes the full $85,000 gain with no offset because the inventory loss is not recognized under lower of cost or market until a triggering event — the hedge produces a $5,000 net earnings impact versus $85,000 without the hedge designation", "Both scenarios produce identical $85,000 gains in net income because inventory is already carried at fair value under GAAP", "In Scenario A, net income includes $0 because fair value hedges eliminate all gains and losses; in Scenario B, net income includes $85,000", "In Scenario A, the $85,000 gain is deferred in OCI; in Scenario B, it is recognized in earnings"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under ASC 815 fair value hedge accounting, both the derivative and the hedged item''s change in fair value attributable to the hedged risk are recognized in earnings. The derivative''s $85,000 gain and the inventory''s $80,000 loss (a basis adjustment to the hedged item) flow through the income statement, producing a net $5,000 impact. The small difference reflects hedge ineffectiveness. In Scenario B (undesignated), the $85,000 derivative gain hits earnings, but inventory under normal GAAP is carried at cost (or lower of cost or NRV under ASC 330) — the $80,000 value decline may not be recognized unless NRV falls below cost. This creates significant earnings volatility: a $85,000 gain with no recognized offset. Fair value hedge accounting solves this by forcing the hedged item to also adjust through earnings, matching the derivative''s impact. (B) is wrong because inventory is not carried at fair value under GAAP. (C) is wrong because fair value hedges do not eliminate gains — they offset them. (D) is wrong because fair value hedge gains are recognized in earnings, not OCI (OCI treatment applies to cash flow hedges).',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 3195;

-- ID 3205: Derivatives — Embedded derivative bifurcation analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Northpoint Corp. issues a bond that contains an embedded equity conversion feature. In Scenario A, the host contract is a debt instrument and the embedded feature allows conversion to equity (not clearly and closely related to debt). In Scenario B, the host contract is a variable-rate note and the embedded feature is an interest rate floor (clearly and closely related to the host). Analyze under ASC 815 why bifurcation is required in one scenario but not the other, and compare the financial statement effects.',
  choices = '["Bifurcation is required in both scenarios because all embedded features must be separated from their host contracts under ASC 815", "Bifurcation is required in Scenario B but not Scenario A because equity features embedded in debt are always excluded from derivative accounting", "Neither scenario requires bifurcation because ASC 815 exempts all embedded derivatives when the host is a financial instrument", "Bifurcation is required in Scenario A because the equity conversion feature is not clearly and closely related to the debt host — the embedded derivative must be separated, measured at fair value, and marked to market through earnings each period; bifurcation is not required in Scenario B because the interest rate floor is clearly and closely related to the variable-rate debt host, so the entire instrument is accounted for as a single unit — this creates income statement volatility only for Scenario A"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): ASC 815-15 requires bifurcation of an embedded derivative when three conditions are met: (1) the hybrid instrument is not measured at fair value with changes in earnings, (2) the embedded feature would qualify as a derivative on a standalone basis, and (3) the embedded feature is not clearly and closely related to the host. In Scenario A, the equity conversion feature in a debt host fails the "clearly and closely related" test because equity risk is fundamentally different from credit/interest risk of the debt host — so bifurcation is required, creating a separate derivative liability measured at fair value through earnings. In Scenario B, the interest rate floor in a variable-rate note is clearly and closely related to the host because both involve interest rate risk — no bifurcation is needed, and the instrument is accounted for as a unit. The practical effect is that Scenario A introduces fair value volatility in earnings from the bifurcated derivative, while Scenario B avoids this complexity. (A) is wrong because the "clearly and closely related" test specifically exempts some embedded features. (B) reverses the analysis. (C) is wrong because ASC 815 does not provide a blanket exemption for financial instrument hosts.',
  correct_index = 3
WHERE id = 3205;

