-- ============================================================

-- ID 663: Revenue — SSP revision impact on allocation analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Pinnacle Software sells a three-element bundle for $300,000: a software license (SSP $180,000), implementation services (SSP $90,000), and a 2-year support contract (SSP $30,000). After a pricing study, Pinnacle revises the SSP for implementation services from $90,000 to $150,000. Analyze how this SSP revision shifts revenue allocation among the performance obligations for new contracts, and determine whether Pinnacle must restate prior period contracts under ASC 606.',
  choices = '["The SSP revision requires restatement of all prior contracts because ASC 606 mandates retrospective application whenever standalone selling prices change", "After the revision, the total SSP pool increases from $300,000 to $360,000, shifting allocation: the software license drops from 60% to 50% ($150,000), implementation rises from 30% to 41.7% ($125,000), and support drops from 10% to 8.3% ($25,000) — prior period contracts are NOT restated because ASC 606 applies the transaction price allocation at contract inception using the SSPs available at that time, and updated SSPs apply only to new contracts", "The SSP revision has no effect on any contract because standalone selling prices are set at the entity level and cannot change once established", "The SSP revision increases total revenue from $300,000 to $360,000 because higher SSPs increase the transaction price"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 606-10-32-33, the transaction price is allocated to performance obligations based on relative standalone selling prices at contract inception. With the revised SSPs ($180,000 + $150,000 + $30,000 = $360,000 pool), the allocation shifts: software license receives $150,000 ($180K/$360K x $300K = 50%), implementation receives $125,000 ($150K/$360K x $300K = 41.7%), and support receives $25,000 ($30K/$360K x $300K = 8.3%). Critically, prior period contracts are not restated — ASC 606 uses the SSPs available at the time the original contract was executed. The revised SSPs apply only to contracts entered into after the pricing study. This protects previously recognized revenue from retroactive adjustment while ensuring new contracts reflect current market pricing. (A) is wrong because ASC 606 does not require retroactive restatement for SSP changes. (C) is wrong because SSPs must be updated to reflect current observable evidence. (D) is wrong because the transaction price ($300,000) is determined by the contract terms, not by SSPs — SSPs only determine how the fixed transaction price is allocated.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 663;

-- ID 665: Revenue — Franchise license point-in-time vs over-time analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'FastTrack Burgers grants a 10-year franchise license and the franchisor conducts nationwide advertising, sets menu standards, and provides ongoing operational support that significantly affects the brand''s value. GreenLeaf Smoothies grants a 10-year franchise license but provides only an initial training manual with no ongoing involvement. Analyze how the nature of the franchisor''s ongoing activities determines whether the franchise license revenue is recognized at a point in time or over time under ASC 606, and compare the revenue patterns.',
  choices = '["FastTrack recognizes the license fee at a point in time because the license is transferred at signing, while GreenLeaf recognizes over time because it has fewer ongoing obligations to fulfill", "Both franchisors recognize revenue at a point in time because franchise licenses always represent rights to use intellectual property at a specific point", "FastTrack recognizes the license fee over the 10-year term because its ongoing activities (advertising, menu standards, operational support) significantly affect the utility of the intellectual property, making it a right to access — GreenLeaf recognizes at a point in time because the absence of ongoing activities means the franchisee controls a static right to use the IP from the grant date, and the license does not change based on the franchisor''s actions", "Both franchisors recognize revenue over time because all franchise agreements involve multi-year terms that require over-time recognition"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under ASC 606-10-55-58 through 55-61, a license of intellectual property is recognized over time (access model) when the entity''s activities significantly affect the utility of the IP to which the customer has rights, the customer is exposed to those effects, and the activities do not transfer a separate good or service. FastTrack''s ongoing advertising, menu standards, and operational support meet all three criteria — the franchisee is paying for access to a continuously evolving brand, so revenue is recognized ratably over 10 years. GreenLeaf provides only static IP (a manual) with no ongoing involvement, so the license is a right to use IP at a point in time — revenue is recognized when the franchisee can direct the use of and obtain substantially all benefits from the license, typically at grant. (A) reverses the analysis entirely. (B) is wrong because franchise licenses can be either access or use depending on the franchisor''s ongoing involvement. (D) is wrong because the multi-year term alone does not determine over-time recognition — the nature of the franchisor''s ongoing activities does.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 665;

-- ID 668: Revenue — Goodwill impairment one-step test analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Hargrove Industries determines that its reporting unit has a carrying amount of $18.5 million (including $2.5 million of allocated goodwill) and a fair value of $17 million. Analyze the impairment loss under the current ASC 350 one-step quantitative test, and explain how this approach differs from the previous two-step method that was eliminated by ASU 2017-04.',
  choices = '["The impairment loss is $2.5 million because the entire goodwill balance is written off whenever the reporting unit''s carrying amount exceeds fair value", "The impairment loss is $0 because the reporting unit''s fair value exceeds its net identifiable assets", "The impairment loss is $17 million because impairment is measured as the reporting unit''s fair value, not the excess", "The impairment loss is $1.5 million — the excess of carrying amount ($18.5M) over fair value ($17M) — capped at the goodwill balance of $2.5 million; under the previous two-step method, the company would have performed an implied fair value calculation of goodwill (similar to a hypothetical purchase price allocation) which could have produced a different impairment amount, but the simplified one-step test eliminates this complexity by measuring impairment as the straightforward excess of carrying amount over fair value"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): Under the current ASC 350-20-35-3C (as amended by ASU 2017-04), goodwill impairment equals the excess of the reporting unit''s carrying amount over its fair value, limited to the goodwill balance. Hargrove''s excess is $18.5M - $17M = $1.5M, which is less than the $2.5M goodwill balance, so the impairment loss is $1.5M. Under the previous two-step method, Step 1 identified impairment (carrying amount > fair value) and Step 2 measured it by calculating the implied fair value of goodwill through a hypothetical purchase price allocation of the reporting unit''s fair value to all assets and liabilities. The implied goodwill (residual) was compared to recorded goodwill. This process was costly and could produce a different impairment figure than the simplified one-step approach. ASU 2017-04 eliminated Step 2 to reduce complexity while still capturing economic impairment. (A) is wrong because impairment is limited to the excess, not the full goodwill balance. (B) is wrong because the comparison is carrying amount vs. fair value of the reporting unit, not vs. net identifiable assets. (C) confuses impairment measurement with the fair value itself.',
  correct_index = 3
WHERE id = 668;

-- ID 670: Revenue — Long-lived asset impairment trigger and two-step process analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Bridgeport Corp. operates a manufacturing plant with a carrying amount of $4.2 million. Due to a major customer''s bankruptcy and a significant decline in product demand, Bridgeport''s management tests the asset for impairment under ASC 360. The undiscounted future cash flows are estimated at $3.8 million, and the fair value is $3.1 million. Analyze which events triggered the impairment test, apply the two-step process, and explain why the asset fails the recoverability test yet still may not require a write-down in different circumstances.',
  choices = '["The customer bankruptcy and demand decline are triggering events that require testing; Step 1 (recoverability) compares carrying amount ($4.2M) to undiscounted cash flows ($3.8M) — since $4.2M > $3.8M, the asset fails; Step 2 measures impairment as carrying amount minus fair value ($4.2M - $3.1M = $1.1M); however, if undiscounted cash flows had been $4.5M instead, the asset would pass Step 1 and no impairment would be recorded despite the fair value being below carrying amount — this recoverability screen prevents impairment on assets that will recover their carrying amount through use", "The impairment loss is $1.1 million ($4.2M - $3.1M) because ASC 360 uses a single-step fair value comparison without a recoverability screen", "No impairment exists because the undiscounted cash flows of $3.8 million are positive, confirming the asset generates value", "The impairment loss is $400,000 ($4.2M - $3.8M) because impairment under ASC 360 is measured as the difference between carrying amount and undiscounted cash flows"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under ASC 360-10-35, impairment testing is triggered by events such as significant adverse changes in the business climate (customer bankruptcy, demand decline). The two-step process first tests recoverability: if the carrying amount ($4.2M) exceeds undiscounted expected future cash flows ($3.8M), the asset is impaired. Step 2 then measures the impairment as carrying amount minus fair value ($4.2M - $3.1M = $1.1M). The recoverability screen is the key analytical insight: if the undiscounted cash flows exceeded the carrying amount (e.g., $4.5M), the asset would pass Step 1 even though fair value ($3.1M) is below carrying amount — no write-down would occur. This design prevents write-downs on assets that will earn back their book value through operations, even if current market value is depressed. (B) is wrong because ASC 360 uses a two-step process, unlike ASC 350''s simplified one-step test for goodwill. (C) is wrong because positive cash flows are insufficient — they must exceed carrying amount to pass recoverability. (D) is wrong because impairment is measured at fair value in Step 2, not at undiscounted cash flows.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 670;

-- ID 3100: Revenue — Transfer of control vs risks and rewards analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Whitfield Equipment delivers a custom machine to a customer on March 1. The customer obtains physical possession and legal title, but Whitfield retains a contractual obligation to bring the machine to specified performance thresholds by June 1 — if the machine fails to meet thresholds, Whitfield must take it back. Under ASC 606''s "transfer of control" framework, revenue may not be recognized at delivery. Analyze how the ASC 606 control framework differs from the legacy "risks and rewards" approach (IAS 18 style), and explain why this scenario could produce different revenue timing conclusions under the two frameworks.',
  choices = '["Both frameworks would defer revenue to June 1 because physical possession without performance guarantees never satisfies either standard", "ASC 606 always recognizes revenue at delivery because physical possession and legal title are conclusive control indicators, while the legacy approach would defer revenue until all risks transfer", "ASC 606 analyzes whether the customer can direct the use of and obtain substantially all remaining benefits from the machine — the performance guarantee creates uncertainty about whether control has transferred because the customer cannot fully direct the asset''s use until acceptance; the legacy risks-and-rewards approach focused on whether significant risks remained with the seller, and both might defer here, but ASC 606 could reach a different conclusion in scenarios where risks transfer but control indicators (like the customer''s ability to direct use) do not align", "The frameworks are identical — ASC 606 simply rebranded ''risks and rewards'' as ''transfer of control'' with no substantive change in outcomes"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): ASC 606-10-25-30 defines transfer of control through five indicators: physical possession, legal title, risks and rewards of ownership, customer acceptance, and present right to payment. However, the overarching principle is whether the customer can direct the use of and obtain substantially all benefits from the asset. In this scenario, the performance guarantee and take-back obligation create doubt about the customer''s ability to fully direct the machine''s use, potentially deferring revenue. The legacy risks-and-rewards model focused primarily on whether significant risks (like return risk) remained with the seller. The frameworks diverge in cases where risks transfer but control does not — for instance, if a customer bears obsolescence risk but cannot use the asset until acceptance. ASC 606''s holistic control analysis can produce different timing than a pure risk-transfer test. (A) is wrong because physical possession is relevant to both frameworks, though not conclusive. (B) is wrong because ASC 606 evaluates all indicators holistically and physical possession alone is not conclusive. (D) is wrong because the two frameworks have substantively different analytical foundations.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 3100;

-- ID 3108: Revenue — Right of return and variable consideration constraint analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Clearwater Products sells $1 million of goods with a right of return. Historical return rates averaged 5% but have recently increased to 12% due to a product quality issue. Analyze how the increase in return rates affects the revenue recognized, the refund liability, and the asset for expected returns under ASC 606, and explain how the variable consideration constraint applies to this situation.',
  choices = '["Revenue is $1 million because the right of return does not affect revenue recognition — returns are accounted for only when they actually occur", "Revenue is $880,000 ($1M x 88%), a refund liability of $120,000 is recorded for expected returns, and an asset is recorded for the cost of inventory expected to be returned — the variable consideration constraint under ASC 606-10-32-11 requires that revenue be recognized only to the extent it is highly probable that a significant reversal will NOT occur, and the elevated 12% return rate means the company must constrain revenue to the amount expected to be retained", "Revenue is $950,000 using the original 5% rate because ASC 606 requires the use of historical averages and prohibits adjusting for recent trends", "Revenue is $0 because any return rate above 10% requires complete deferral of revenue until the return period expires"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 606-10-55-22 through 55-27, sales with a right of return create variable consideration. The entity must estimate the amount of consideration it expects to be entitled to keep using the expected value method. With a 12% return rate, the company expects to retain $880,000 of the $1M. ASC 606-10-32-11 requires the variable consideration constraint: revenue is included only to the extent it is highly probable that a significant revenue reversal will not occur. The rising return rate increases the constraint, reducing recognized revenue from what it would be at 5%. A refund liability of $120,000 represents the obligation to return cash for expected returns, and an asset for the expected inventory returns (at cost, not selling price) is recorded. (A) is wrong because ASC 606 requires estimation of variable consideration at inception, not waiting for actual returns. (C) is wrong because the constraint requires using the best available estimate, including recent trends — relying solely on outdated historical averages when conditions have changed would violate the highly probable threshold. (D) is wrong because no bright-line threshold exists; the constraint is principles-based, not rule-based.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 3108;

-- ============================================================
-- FINANCIAL VALUATION METHODS (6 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 640: Valuation — FCFF impact of capital expenditure increase on enterprise value
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ridgeline Corp. currently generates FCFF of $12 million with capital expenditures of $8 million. The company plans to increase capex by 50% to $12 million to expand production capacity, with no immediate change in operating cash flow. Analyze how this increase affects FCFF and the resulting enterprise value, and explain why capital-intensive companies typically trade at lower EV/EBITDA multiples than asset-light companies.',
  choices = '["FCFF decreases from $12 million to $8 million because the $4 million capex increase directly reduces free cash flow — capital-intensive companies trade at lower EV/EBITDA multiples because higher ongoing capex requirements mean less of each dollar of EBITDA converts to free cash flow available to investors, making the same EBITDA less valuable on a per-dollar basis", "FCFF is unaffected because capital expenditures are excluded from the free cash flow calculation and only appear on the balance sheet", "FCFF increases because higher capex generates proportionally higher revenue immediately, and capital-intensive companies trade at premium multiples due to their larger asset bases", "FCFF decreases but enterprise value increases because the market rewards companies that invest heavily in growth regardless of current cash flow"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): FCFF = Operating Cash Flow minus Capital Expenditures (simplified). Increasing capex from $8M to $12M reduces FCFF from $12M to $8M (a 33% decline), assuming operating cash flow is unchanged in the near term. In a DCF model, lower FCFF directly reduces enterprise value. Capital-intensive companies trade at lower EV/EBITDA multiples because EBITDA does not capture capex — two companies with identical EBITDA but different capex requirements will generate different free cash flows. An asset-light company converting 80% of EBITDA to free cash flow is worth more per dollar of EBITDA than a capital-intensive company converting 40%. This is why valuation analysts adjust EBITDA multiples for capital intensity or prefer FCFF-based valuation. (B) is wrong because capex is a direct deduction in the FCFF formula. (C) is wrong because capex expansion does not produce immediate revenue increases and capital-intensive firms generally trade at lower, not higher, multiples. (D) is wrong because markets do not uniformly reward capex spending — value creation depends on whether returns on invested capital exceed the cost of capital.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 640;

-- ID 641: Valuation — Gordon Growth Model sensitivity to growth rate changes
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'An analyst values a stock using the Gordon Growth Model with a cost of equity of 12% and a growth rate of 7%, producing an intrinsic value of $40 per share. The analyst then increases the growth rate assumption from 7% to 8%. Analyze the sensitivity of the intrinsic value to this 1-percentage-point change, and explain the mathematical reason why a small increase in the growth rate produces a disproportionately large change in value when the growth rate approaches the discount rate.',
  choices = '["The value increases from $40.00 to $42.00 — a modest 5% increase proportional to the 1-percentage-point change in growth — because the Gordon Growth Model produces linear sensitivity to changes in growth rates", "The value increases from $40.00 to $50.00 — a 25% increase from a 1-percentage-point growth change — because the model divides by (k - g), and as g approaches k, the denominator shrinks toward zero, causing the value to increase exponentially; at g = 7%, the denominator is 0.05 (5%), but at g = 8%, it shrinks to 0.04 (4%), a 20% reduction in the denominator that produces a 25% increase in value", "The value decreases because higher growth rates increase risk, which the model penalizes through a higher discount rate", "The value is unchanged because the Gordon Growth Model only responds to changes in the discount rate, not the growth rate"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The Gordon Growth Model formula is V = D1 / (k - g). At g = 7% and k = 12%, the denominator is 5%. The implied D1 is $40 x 0.05 = $2.00. At g = 8%, the denominator becomes 4%, and V = $2.00 x (1.08/1.07) / 0.04 = approximately $50.00 (the slight D1 adjustment from higher growth makes the exact figure close to $50.47, but the dominant effect is the denominator change). The key insight is the nonlinear sensitivity: as g approaches k, the denominator approaches zero and value approaches infinity. This is why the Gordon Growth Model is highly sensitive to the growth rate assumption and why analysts must exercise caution — a 1-point change in g produced a 25% change in value. This sensitivity also explains why the model is unreliable when g is close to k. (A) is wrong because the relationship is hyperbolic, not linear. (C) is wrong because the growth rate and discount rate are separate inputs in the model; higher growth does not automatically increase the discount rate. (D) is wrong because the growth rate appears in the denominator and directly affects value.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 641;

-- ID 643: Valuation — EV/EBITDA vs P/E comparison for acquisition analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'An acquirer is evaluating two targets: Ironclad Industries (70% debt-financed, 35% effective tax rate) and Freeport Systems (100% equity-financed, 15% effective tax rate due to tax credits). Both companies report identical net income of $10 million. Analyze why the acquirer would prefer EV/EBITDA over P/E as a valuation metric when comparing these two targets, and identify the specific distortions that P/E introduces.',
  choices = '["P/E is preferable because net income reflects the actual returns available to equity holders after all obligations, making it a more conservative metric", "EV/EBITDA is preferable because it strips out the effects of capital structure (interest expense) and tax policy differences — Ironclad''s heavy debt reduces its net income through interest expense, depressing P/E relative to its operating performance, while Freeport''s tax credits inflate net income relative to pre-tax operations; EV/EBITDA measures operating performance before these financing and tax effects, providing a more comparable basis for acquisition valuation", "EV/EBITDA is preferable only because it produces a lower number, making the targets appear cheaper and easier to justify to the board", "Both metrics are equally valid because identical net income means the companies are equally profitable regardless of capital structure or tax rates"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): P/E (Price/Earnings) uses net income, which is affected by capital structure (interest expense reduces earnings for leveraged firms) and tax policy (credits, rates, and jurisdictions). Ironclad''s 70% debt financing creates substantial interest expense that depresses net income relative to operating performance — its P/E appears higher (more expensive) than warranted by operations. Freeport''s tax credits inflate net income relative to pre-tax performance — its P/E appears lower (cheaper) than warranted. EV/EBITDA eliminates both distortions: enterprise value captures total firm value regardless of financing mix, and EBITDA measures operating performance before interest, taxes, depreciation, and amortization. For an acquirer comparing firms with different capital structures and tax situations, EV/EBITDA provides a capital-structure-neutral and tax-neutral comparison. (A) is wrong because P/E''s sensitivity to financing and tax differences makes it less comparable across firms with different structures. (C) is wrong because the preference is based on analytical comparability, not cosmetic appearance. (D) is wrong because identical net income does not mean equal operating profitability when leverage and tax rates differ.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 643;

-- ID 3034: Valuation — ASC 820 fair value hierarchy measurement reliability
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Lakewood Investments holds three assets: a publicly traded stock (valued using quoted market prices), a corporate bond (valued using observable yields on similar bonds), and a private equity stake (valued using a discounted cash flow model with management''s assumptions). Analyze how the level of inputs in the ASC 820 fair value hierarchy affects measurement reliability for each asset, and explain why a Level 3 valuation carries more estimation uncertainty and what additional disclosures are required.',
  choices = '["All three assets have identical measurement reliability because ASC 820 produces the same quality of fair value estimate regardless of the input level", "The stock is Level 3 because equity valuations are inherently uncertain, the bond is Level 2, and the private equity stake is Level 1 because DCF models produce the most precise valuations", "The stock is Level 1 (highest reliability — unadjusted quoted prices in active markets with no estimation needed), the bond is Level 2 (moderate reliability — observable market data requires some adjustment), and the private equity stake is Level 3 (lowest reliability — unobservable inputs require significant management judgment); Level 3 valuations require additional disclosures including a rollforward of balances, quantitative information about significant unobservable inputs, and a sensitivity analysis of how changes in those inputs affect the fair value estimate", "All three assets are Level 2 because none are traded on a dedicated exchange for that specific instrument"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): ASC 820-10-35-37 through 35-54 establishes a three-level hierarchy prioritizing inputs to fair value measurement. Level 1 uses unadjusted quoted prices in active markets (the publicly traded stock) — these are the most reliable because they reflect actual market transactions with no estimation. Level 2 uses observable inputs other than Level 1 prices (the corporate bond valued using comparable bond yields) — reliable but requiring some judgment in selecting and adjusting comparables. Level 3 uses significant unobservable inputs (the private equity stake''s DCF model) — these carry the greatest estimation uncertainty because the fair value depends on management''s assumptions about cash flows, growth rates, and discount rates. ASC 820-10-50-2 requires enhanced disclosures for Level 3 measurements including: beginning/ending balance rollforward, total gains/losses in earnings and OCI, purchases/sales/issuances/settlements, transfers between levels, and quantitative details about unobservable inputs with sensitivity analysis. (A) is wrong because the hierarchy exists specifically to differentiate measurement reliability. (B) reverses the hierarchy. (D) is wrong because the stock''s quoted price on an active exchange meets the Level 1 definition.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 3034;

-- ID 3051: Valuation — FCFF/WACC vs FCFE/cost of equity discrepancy analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'An analyst values Glendale Corp. using two methods: FCFF discounted at WACC produces an equity value of $85 million, while FCFE discounted at the cost of equity produces an equity value of $72 million. Both models use the same 5-year projection period. Analyze when these two approaches would produce different equity values and identify the assumption that most likely causes the $13 million discrepancy.',
  choices = '["The discrepancy is caused by different growth rate assumptions — FCFF models always use higher growth rates than FCFE models, inflating the FCFF-based value", "The two approaches should always produce identical equity values regardless of assumptions, so the $13 million difference must be a calculation error", "The discrepancy most likely arises from inconsistent assumptions about the company''s capital structure over the projection period — FCFF/WACC assumes a constant target debt-to-equity ratio embedded in WACC, while FCFE/cost of equity reflects the actual debt repayment and issuance pattern; if Glendale plans to aggressively pay down debt (reducing leverage below the WACC assumption), the FCFE approach captures this deleveraging while the FCFF approach does not, producing divergent equity values", "The FCFF approach always produces higher values because WACC is always lower than the cost of equity, and a lower discount rate mechanically inflates present values"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): In theory, FCFF discounted at WACC and FCFE discounted at cost of equity should produce identical equity values if assumptions are consistent — particularly the capital structure assumption. WACC uses a target or assumed constant capital structure to weight the cost of debt and equity. If the company''s actual capital structure changes over the projection period (e.g., paying down debt, issuing equity, or increasing leverage), the constant WACC assumption diverges from reality. The FCFE approach directly models cash flows to equity after debt service, capturing the actual debt dynamics. The $13M discrepancy most likely reflects Glendale planning capital structure changes that the WACC-based model does not capture. This is a well-known limitation of the FCFF/WACC approach for companies with changing leverage. (A) is wrong because both models should use the same operational assumptions; the issue is financing assumptions, not growth rates. (B) is wrong because they only produce identical results under perfectly consistent assumptions, which is rare in practice. (D) is wrong because while WACC is typically lower than cost of equity, this is offset by the fact that FCFF is pre-debt-service (higher than FCFE), so the ratio of higher numerator to lower denominator does not mechanically inflate values.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 3051;

-- ID 3059: Valuation — Capitalization rate decrease and enterprise value impact
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Harrington Consulting is valued using the capitalization of earnings method. Its normalized earnings are $1.5 million. The original capitalization rate is 16%, but a reassessment reduces it to 12% due to lower perceived risk. Analyze the effect on the estimated enterprise value and explain the economic reasoning behind why a lower capitalization rate increases value.',
  choices = '["Enterprise value decreases from $9.375 million to $7.5 million because a lower capitalization rate means lower expected earnings capacity", "Enterprise value remains at $9.375 million because the capitalization rate does not affect enterprise value — only earnings determine value", "Enterprise value increases from $9.375 million to $12.5 million — the lower capitalization rate reflects reduced risk, meaning investors require a lower return, which translates to a higher price they are willing to pay; economically, a less risky earnings stream is more valuable because each dollar of expected earnings carries greater certainty of realization, so investors capitalize those earnings at a lower rate, paying a premium for predictability", "Enterprise value doubles from $9.375 million to $18.75 million because the capitalization rate was cut in half"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): The capitalization of earnings formula is: Enterprise Value = Normalized Earnings / Capitalization Rate. At 16%: $1.5M / 0.16 = $9.375M. At 12%: $1.5M / 0.12 = $12.5M — a 33% increase from a 4-percentage-point reduction in the capitalization rate. The economic reasoning: the capitalization rate represents the required rate of return investors demand to hold this investment. A lower rate means investors perceive less risk (more stable earnings, stronger competitive position, lower business volatility), so they accept a lower return. Since they demand less return per dollar of earnings, they are willing to pay a higher price — the same $1.5M earnings stream is worth more when it is perceived as more certain. This is the inverse relationship between risk and value that underlies all income-based valuation. (A) is wrong because a lower capitalization rate increases value, not decreases it. (B) is wrong because the capitalization rate directly affects value as the denominator. (D) is wrong mathematically — the rate decreased from 16% to 12% (a 25% reduction), not by half, and value increased by 33%, not 100%.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 3059;

