-- ID 692: Consolidations — Intercompany profit elimination with 80% vs 100% ownership
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Dawson Corp. (parent) sells inventory to its subsidiary, Trent Inc., at a 37.5% markup on cost. The inventory cost Dawson $160,000, and it was sold to Trent for $220,000. At year-end, 25% of this inventory remains unsold in Trent''s warehouse. Dawson owns 80% of Trent. Analyze how the elimination of unrealized intercompany profit differs compared to a scenario where Dawson owns 100% of Trent, and explain how noncontrolling interest is affected.',
  choices = '["At 80% ownership, only 80% of the unrealized profit ($12,000) is eliminated, while at 100% ownership the full $15,000 is eliminated — the noncontrolling interest absorbs the remaining $3,000 at the 80% level", "The full $15,000 of unrealized profit is eliminated at both ownership levels because the consolidated entity cannot report profit on internal transactions — at 80% ownership, the entire $15,000 elimination is charged against the parent''s share of consolidated earnings because this is a downstream sale (parent to subsidiary), so the NCI is unaffected; at 100% there is no NCI to consider", "No elimination is necessary at either ownership level because the inventory is a legitimate third-party transaction once the subsidiary takes legal title", "At 80% ownership, the unrealized profit is $60,000 and is allocated between the parent and NCI; at 100% ownership, the profit is $0 because wholly-owned subsidiaries cannot engage in intercompany transactions"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The unrealized intercompany profit on the unsold inventory is $15,000 (25% of the $60,000 total markup, where $60,000 = $220,000 - $160,000). In consolidation, the full $15,000 must be eliminated regardless of ownership percentage because consolidated statements report the economic entity as if intercompany sales never occurred. At both 80% and 100% ownership, the same $15,000 is removed from ending inventory and cost of goods sold. The critical analytical distinction is the NCI impact: since this is a downstream sale (parent sold to subsidiary), the unrealized profit originated with the parent. Under current GAAP, the full downstream elimination is attributed to the parent''s share — NCI is unaffected because NCI did not earn any of the intercompany profit. If this were an upstream sale (subsidiary to parent), NCI would absorb its proportionate share (20% of $15,000 = $3,000). (A) is wrong because partial elimination is not permitted — the full unrealized profit is always eliminated. (C) is wrong because intercompany transactions must be eliminated regardless of legal form. (D) is wrong on both the profit calculation and the premise.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 692;

-- ID 695: Consolidations — Upstream vs downstream intercompany equipment sale comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'In Scenario A, a parent company sells equipment to its 80%-owned subsidiary (downstream) for $500,000 when the book value is $350,000 — the subsidiary depreciates it over 5 remaining years. In Scenario B, the same 80%-owned subsidiary sells identical equipment to the parent (upstream) under the same terms. Compare the consolidation effects of these two scenarios, and analyze why the depreciation adjustment pattern is the same but the NCI impact differs.',
  choices = '["In both scenarios, the $150,000 intercompany gain is eliminated and $30,000 of excess depreciation is reversed annually, restoring the asset to its original basis over 5 years — the difference is in who bears the elimination: in the downstream sale (Scenario A), the full gain elimination and depreciation reversal are charged against the parent''s earnings because the parent originated the gain, leaving NCI unaffected; in the upstream sale (Scenario B), NCI absorbs 20% of both the gain elimination and the depreciation adjustment because the subsidiary originated the gain", "The upstream sale requires no consolidation adjustments because the parent is receiving an asset from a subsidiary it controls", "Downstream sales never require elimination because the parent is the controlling entity, while upstream sales require full elimination", "The depreciation adjustment differs between upstream and downstream because the asset''s useful life changes depending on the direction of the intercompany transfer"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): In both scenarios, the consolidation mechanics are identical: the $150,000 intercompany gain is eliminated by reducing the equipment to its $350,000 original cost, and the $30,000 annual excess depreciation ($150,000 / 5 years) is reversed because the consolidated entity should depreciate only from the original basis. The direction of the sale does not change these amounts. However, the allocation between controlling and noncontrolling interests differs. In a downstream sale, the parent originated the gain, so the entire elimination reduces the parent''s share of consolidated income — NCI reports its normal share of the subsidiary''s independently earned income. In an upstream sale, the subsidiary originated the gain, so NCI (at 20%) absorbs its share of the elimination: 20% of the $150,000 gain reversal ($30,000) and 20% of each $30,000 depreciation adjustment ($6,000 annually). (B) is wrong because upstream sales require the same consolidation adjustments. (C) is wrong because both directions require elimination. (D) is wrong because the asset''s useful life and depreciation pattern are the same regardless of transfer direction.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 695;

-- ID 696: Consolidations — Partial disposal retaining control as equity transaction
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Sterling Corp. owns 80% of Vista Holdings and decides to sell 20% of Vista''s shares to an outside investor, reducing its ownership from 80% to 60%. Sterling retains control. The carrying amount of NCI before the sale is $4 million. The sale generates $6 million in cash, and the proportionate book value of the shares sold is $4.5 million. Analyze why ASC 810 treats this as an equity transaction rather than a gain/loss event, and explain the effect on additional paid-in capital.',
  choices = '["Sterling recognizes a $1.5 million gain ($6M cash minus $4.5M book value) in the income statement because any sale of subsidiary shares is a disposal that generates gain or loss", "The transaction is recorded by reducing Sterling''s investment in Vista by $6 million and recognizing a loss of $1.5 million", "ASC 810 requires deconsolidation whenever any shares are sold, so Sterling must remove Vista from consolidated statements and report the remaining 60% as an equity method investment", "ASC 810 treats this as an equity transaction because Sterling retains control — when the controlling interest does not change, changes in ownership are transactions among equity holders, not gain/loss events; the $1.5 million excess of cash received ($6M) over the adjustment to NCI ($4.5M) is credited to additional paid-in capital (APIC) — no gain or loss is recognized in the income statement because the consolidated entity has not disposed of a business, only reallocated ownership among its equity participants"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): Under ASC 810-10-45-23, changes in a parent''s ownership interest while retaining control are equity transactions. The rationale is that from a consolidated entity perspective, no disposal has occurred — the parent still controls the subsidiary, and the consolidated entity simply has a different split between controlling and noncontrolling interests. The accounting: NCI increases by $4.5 million (proportionate book value transferred), cash increases by $6 million, and the $1.5 million difference is credited to APIC within equity. No gain or loss is recognized in consolidated net income. This treatment reflects the entity theory of consolidation — the transaction occurs among owners of the entity, similar to a parent issuing or repurchasing its own shares. (A) is wrong because gain/loss recognition occurs only when control is lost, not when ownership changes within control. (B) is wrong about both the direction and the recognition of a loss. (C) is wrong because deconsolidation occurs only when control is lost — 60% ownership with control still requires full consolidation.',
  correct_index = 3
WHERE id = 696;

-- ID 3162: Consolidations — Downstream intercompany profit effect on NCI
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Apex Corp. (parent, 80% ownership) sells inventory to its subsidiary, Zenith Inc., recognizing a $100,000 intercompany profit. At year-end, all inventory remains unsold by Zenith. Analyze the effect of eliminating the $100,000 downstream intercompany profit on consolidated net income, the parent''s share, and NCI''s share, and explain why the full $100,000 is eliminated even though NCI owns 20% of the subsidiary.',
  choices = '["Only $80,000 is eliminated (parent''s 80% share) because NCI''s 20% share represents a legitimate arm''s-length portion of the transaction", "The full $100,000 is eliminated and allocated proportionally: $80,000 reduces the parent''s share and $20,000 reduces NCI''s share, because all intercompany eliminations are split by ownership percentage", "The full $100,000 is eliminated from consolidated income because the consolidated entity cannot report profit on a transaction with itself — the entire elimination is charged to the parent''s share of consolidated earnings because Apex (the parent) originated the downstream sale, and NCI''s share of Zenith''s independently earned income is unaffected by a transaction the subsidiary did not initiate", "No elimination is needed because the inventory was purchased at market price by the subsidiary, making it a fair-value transaction"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): In consolidation, all intercompany profits must be eliminated in full, regardless of NCI ownership. The $100,000 is removed because, from the consolidated entity''s perspective, no sale occurred — inventory merely moved between divisions. The critical analytical point is the allocation of the elimination: because this is a downstream sale (parent to subsidiary), the parent generated the intercompany profit. Under current GAAP (ASC 810), the full elimination reduces the parent''s share of consolidated net income by $100,000. NCI''s share of the subsidiary''s earnings is not adjusted because NCI did not participate in generating the intercompany profit — the subsidiary simply purchased inventory and has not resold it. This differs from an upstream sale, where NCI would absorb its proportionate share of the elimination because the subsidiary originated the profit. (A) is wrong because partial elimination is never permitted in consolidation. (B) is wrong because the allocation follows the direction of the sale (downstream = all to parent), not the ownership split. (D) is wrong because intercompany transactions are eliminated regardless of pricing.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 3162;

-- ID 3166: Consolidations — NCI share with upstream intercompany adjustment
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Beacon Corp. owns 80% of Dalton Industries, which reports net income of $500,000 for the year. Included in Dalton''s income is $80,000 of unrealized upstream intercompany profit on inventory sold to Beacon that remains unsold at year-end. Analyze NCI''s reported share of Dalton''s income with and without the intercompany adjustment, and explain the rationale for adjusting NCI''s share.',
  choices = '["Without the adjustment, NCI''s share is $100,000 (20% x $500,000); with the adjustment, NCI''s share is $84,000 (20% x $420,000) — the $16,000 reduction occurs because the upstream intercompany profit originated from Dalton, and NCI as an owner of Dalton shares proportionally in both the subsidiary''s legitimate earnings and the required elimination of its intercompany profits", "NCI''s share is $100,000 in both cases because NCI is never affected by intercompany eliminations", "NCI''s share is $0 because all intercompany profits must be allocated entirely to the controlling interest regardless of direction", "Without the adjustment, NCI''s share is $16,000 (20% x $80,000); with the adjustment, NCI''s share is $84,000"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Without any intercompany adjustment, NCI would report $100,000 (20% x $500,000 reported income). However, the $80,000 of upstream intercompany profit is unrealized because the inventory remains unsold to third parties. Since Dalton originated this profit (upstream sale), the elimination affects Dalton''s adjusted income: $500,000 - $80,000 = $420,000. NCI''s adjusted share is 20% x $420,000 = $84,000. The rationale is that NCI, as an equity holder in the subsidiary that created the intercompany profit, must share proportionally in the elimination of that profit. The $16,000 reduction ($100,000 - $84,000 = 20% x $80,000) ensures NCI does not benefit from unrealized profits that the consolidated entity has not earned from external parties. This contrasts with downstream sales where NCI is unaffected because the parent originated the profit. (B) is wrong because NCI is affected by upstream intercompany eliminations. (C) is wrong because upstream eliminations are shared proportionally, not allocated entirely to the parent. (D) confuses the computation method.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 3166;

-- ID 3168: Consolidations — Intercompany dividend elimination across financial statements
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A parent company owns 100% of a subsidiary that declares and pays $200,000 in dividends. Analyze why these intercompany dividends are eliminated in consolidation, and compare how the elimination affects the consolidated income statement, balance sheet, and statement of cash flows.',
  choices = '["Intercompany dividends are not eliminated because they represent actual cash transfers between legal entities that must be reported in consolidated statements", "Only the income statement effect is eliminated; the cash flow statement must show the full $200,000 as an investing activity", "Intercompany dividends are eliminated because, from the consolidated entity''s perspective, a company cannot pay dividends to itself — on the income statement, any dividend income recorded by the parent is reversed; on the balance sheet, the parent''s dividend receivable (if any) and subsidiary''s dividend payable are eliminated; on the statement of cash flows, the $200,000 is excluded because it is an internal transfer that does not represent cash flowing into or out of the consolidated entity", "The dividends are eliminated on the balance sheet only — the income statement and cash flow statement report them as external transactions"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): In consolidation, the parent and subsidiary are treated as a single economic entity. Intercompany dividends are internal transfers — the consolidated entity cannot distribute dividends to itself. On the income statement, any dividend income the parent recognized is eliminated because it does not represent earnings from external parties. On the balance sheet, any intercompany receivable (parent) and payable (subsidiary) are eliminated, and the reduction in the subsidiary''s retained earnings is offset by the parent''s receipt. On the cash flow statement, the $200,000 is excluded because it represents cash moving from one pocket to another within the same entity — only dividends paid to external shareholders (including NCI) appear on the consolidated statement. (A) is wrong because the fundamental purpose of consolidation is to eliminate intercompany transactions that do not represent external economic activity. (B) is wrong because including the dividend on the cash flow statement would overstate cash flows by showing internal transfers as external activity. (D) is wrong because elimination is comprehensive across all three statements.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 3168;

-- ID 3176: Consolidations — Intercompany receivable/payable elimination and ratio effects
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'At year-end, a parent has a $750,000 receivable from its subsidiary, and the subsidiary has a corresponding $750,000 payable to the parent. Before consolidation, the combined total assets of both entities are $25 million and combined total liabilities are $15 million. Analyze the effect of eliminating these intercompany balances on the consolidated balance sheet, and explain how failure to eliminate would overstate key financial ratios.',
  choices = '["Elimination reduces consolidated total assets and total liabilities each by $750,000 (to $24.25M and $14.25M respectively) — failure to eliminate would overstate both sides of the balance sheet, inflating the debt-to-assets ratio from 58.8% to 60.0% and misrepresenting the entity''s true leverage; additionally, receivable turnover and payable metrics would be distorted by including amounts that never involve external parties", "Elimination has no effect on the balance sheet because the receivable and payable cancel each other mathematically and are automatically excluded", "Elimination increases consolidated total assets because removing the payable frees up resources for the subsidiary", "Elimination only affects the income statement through interest income and expense; the balance sheet balances remain as reported"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): The intercompany receivable ($750,000 asset on the parent''s books) and payable ($750,000 liability on the subsidiary''s books) must be eliminated because they represent obligations between entities within the consolidated group, not with external parties. Elimination reduces total assets from $25M to $24.25M and total liabilities from $15M to $14.25M. The debt-to-assets ratio drops from $15M/$25M = 60.0% to $14.25M/$24.25M = 58.8%. Without elimination, the consolidated entity would appear to have more assets and more liabilities than it actually owes to or is owed by external parties, overstating leverage. Additionally, accounts receivable turnover would be understated (inflated receivables denominator) and days payable would be distorted. These intercompany balances have no economic substance at the consolidated level because one entity''s asset is another entity''s liability within the same group. (B) is wrong because elimination requires an active journal entry in the consolidation worksheet — it is not automatic. (C) is wrong because elimination reduces, not increases, consolidated assets. (D) is wrong because the balance sheet effect is the primary concern in intercompany balance elimination.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 3176;

