-- ID 1076: Leases — Repurchase option effect on sale-leaseback qualification
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Prescott Corp. sells a building to an investor for $2 million and immediately leases it back. In Scenario A, the leaseback includes a repurchase option at fair market value. In Scenario B, the repurchase option is at a fixed price of $1.2 million, which is expected to be significantly below market value at the exercise date. Analyze under ASC 842 how the repurchase option affects whether the transfer qualifies as a sale, and compare the accounting treatment in each scenario.',
  choices = '["Both scenarios qualify as sales because the legal transfer of title is the sole criterion for sale recognition under ASC 842", "In Scenario A, the transfer qualifies as a sale because a fair-value repurchase option does not give the seller-lessee effective control over the asset — the buyer-lessor bears the risk of value changes; in Scenario B, the transfer does NOT qualify as a sale because the below-market repurchase option gives the seller-lessee a significant economic incentive to reacquire the asset, indicating the buyer-lessor does not obtain control — the transaction is treated as a financing arrangement with the $2M recorded as a financial liability", "In Scenario A, the transfer does not qualify as a sale but Scenario B does, because below-market options are treated as purchase commitments that transfer control to the buyer", "Neither scenario qualifies as a sale because any repurchase option, regardless of terms, disqualifies the transfer under ASC 842"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 842-40-25-3, a transfer of an asset followed by a leaseback is evaluated under ASC 606''s control transfer criteria. A repurchase option at fair market value (Scenario A) does not prevent sale recognition because the buyer-lessor bears the residual value risk — the seller-lessee would only exercise at FMV, which provides no economic advantage over purchasing a similar asset on the open market. A below-market repurchase option (Scenario B) creates a significant economic incentive for the seller-lessee to reacquire the asset, indicating the buyer-lessor has not obtained control. Under ASC 606-10-25-27, this is treated as if control never transferred, and the transaction is accounted for as a financing: the $2M proceeds become a financial liability, and the building remains on Prescott''s books. (A) is wrong because legal title alone does not determine sale recognition — economic substance (control transfer) governs. (C) reverses the analysis. (D) is wrong because fair-value repurchase options do not disqualify the transfer.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1076;

-- ID 1078: Leases — Lease modification separate contract vs remeasurement analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'In Scenario A, Carver Corp. modifies a lease to add a second floor of office space at a standalone price, increasing total consideration proportionally. In Scenario B, Carver modifies a lease to extend the term by 3 years on the existing space without adding new scope, and the revised payments are below market rates. Analyze why ASC 842 treats these two modifications differently, and compare the effect on the right-of-use asset.',
  choices = '["Both modifications are treated as new separate contracts because any change to a lease constitutes a new agreement under ASC 842", "Scenario A is treated as a separate contract because it adds scope (new right-of-use asset) at a commensurate standalone price — the existing lease is unaffected and a new ROU asset is recognized for the additional space; Scenario B is NOT a separate contract because extending the term without adding scope does not qualify — instead, the existing lease is remeasured, updating the lease liability at the revised discount rate and adjusting the ROU asset accordingly, which changes the lessee''s balance sheet and expense pattern going forward", "Scenario A requires remeasurement while Scenario B is treated as a separate contract, because term extensions always create separate contracts", "Neither modification affects the ROU asset because lease modifications only impact the lease liability"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 842-10-25-8, a lease modification is treated as a separate contract if it grants an additional right of use (new scope) and the increase in consideration is commensurate with the standalone price of the new scope. Scenario A meets both criteria: the second floor is a new ROU asset, and the pricing is at standalone rates. The original lease continues unchanged, and a new ROU asset and liability are recognized for the additional space. Scenario B does not add scope — it merely extends the existing right of use at below-market rates. Since the consideration is not commensurate with a standalone extension, this is not a separate contract. Instead, Carver remeasures the lease liability using the revised payments and a new discount rate, and adjusts the ROU asset by the same amount of the liability change. This changes the balance sheet carrying amounts and the prospective expense recognition pattern. (A) is wrong because ASC 842 distinguishes between modifications that qualify as separate contracts and those requiring remeasurement. (C) reverses the treatment. (D) is wrong because remeasurement adjusts both the liability and the ROU asset.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1078;

-- ID 1079: Leases — Sale-leaseback gain recognition at fair value vs above fair value
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'In Scenario A, Dalton Manufacturing sells a factory with a carrying value of $500,000 and a fair value of $750,000 for exactly $750,000, then leases it back at market rates. In Scenario B, Dalton sells the identical factory for $850,000 (above fair value) and leases it back at above-market rental rates. Both transfers qualify as sales under ASC 842. Analyze the gain recognition pattern in each scenario and explain how ASC 842 treats the $100,000 excess above fair value in Scenario B.',
  choices = '["In Scenario A, Dalton recognizes a $250,000 gain ($750K - $500K) immediately because the sale price equals fair value and leaseback terms are at market; in Scenario B, only $250,000 of gain is recognized immediately (the portion representing the genuine sale at fair value) while the $100,000 excess of sale price over fair value is treated as additional financing from the buyer-lessor — this $100,000 is recorded as a financial liability and amortized over the lease term because it represents prepaid rent, not a true sale premium", "In Scenario A, Dalton recognizes a $250,000 gain immediately; in Scenario B, Dalton recognizes a $350,000 gain immediately — because ASC 842 requires full gain recognition on all qualifying sale-leaseback transactions regardless of pricing", "In both scenarios, all gains are deferred and amortized over the leaseback term because ASC 842 prohibits immediate gain recognition on sale-leaseback transactions", "In Scenario A, no gain is recognized because sale-leasebacks at fair value produce zero gain; in Scenario B, the full $350,000 gain is recognized because the above-market price proves the asset was undervalued"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under ASC 842-40-25-4, when the sale price equals fair value, the seller-lessee recognizes the full gain on the difference between carrying amount and fair value. In Scenario A: $750,000 - $500,000 = $250,000 gain recognized immediately. When the sale price exceeds fair value (Scenario B), ASC 842-40-30-1 requires bifurcation: the $250,000 representing the genuine fair-value sale is recognized as a gain, while the $100,000 excess ($850,000 - $750,000) is accounted for as additional financing — effectively a loan from the buyer-lessor repaid through the above-market lease payments. The $100,000 is recorded as a financial liability and reduced over the lease term as the above-market rent payments are made. This prevents seller-lessees from inflating sale gains by negotiating above-market sale prices offset by above-market lease payments. (B) is wrong because ASC 842 does not permit recognition of the excess above fair value as a gain. (C) is wrong because ASC 842 generally permits immediate gain recognition at fair value, unlike the old ASC 840. (D) is wrong because a fair-value sale does produce a gain equal to the FV minus carrying amount.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 1079;

-- ID 3271: Leases — Short-term lease exemption trade-off analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Waverly Corp. enters a 10-month equipment lease with monthly payments of $5,000. The company is deciding whether to elect the short-term lease exemption under ASC 842. Analyze the trade-offs of electing vs. not electing the exemption, and compare the balance sheet and income statement effects under each approach. Explain when the exemption is advantageous.',
  choices = '["Electing the exemption is always disadvantageous because it prevents the company from recognizing an asset on the balance sheet, weakening its financial position", "Without the exemption, Waverly recognizes a right-of-use asset and lease liability of approximately $48,000 on the balance sheet and records amortization plus interest expense (front-loaded pattern); with the exemption, neither asset nor liability appears on the balance sheet and the $5,000 monthly payment is recognized as straight-line rent expense — the exemption is advantageous when the administrative cost of measurement and tracking outweighs the benefit, and when the company wants to minimize reported liabilities and maintain simpler financial statements", "Electing the exemption increases total expense over the lease term because the IRS imposes a penalty for companies that do not capitalize short-term leases", "Both approaches produce identical balance sheet and income statement results because ASC 842 treats all leases under 12 months as operating leases with the same recognition pattern"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 842-20-25-2, a lessee may elect (by class of underlying asset) to exclude short-term leases (12 months or less with no purchase option the lessee is reasonably certain to exercise) from balance sheet recognition. Without the election, the 10-month lease requires recognizing an ROU asset and lease liability based on the present value of the remaining payments (approximately $48,000 depending on the discount rate), with an amortization-plus-interest expense pattern. With the election, no asset or liability is recognized and the $5,000 monthly payments are straight-line rent expense. The exemption is advantageous when: (1) tracking and measuring short-term leases imposes disproportionate administrative burden, (2) the company prefers lower reported liabilities for covenant or optics reasons, and (3) the portfolio of short-term leases is immaterial. Total expense over 10 months is approximately $50,000 under both approaches, but the pattern and balance sheet presentation differ materially. (A) is wrong because avoiding liability recognition can be a strategic advantage. (C) is wrong because the exemption has no tax penalty. (D) is wrong because the balance sheet treatment is fundamentally different with vs. without the exemption.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 3271;

-- ID 3275: Leases — Variable payments tied to index vs usage comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'In Scenario A, a lease requires annual base payments of $100,000 adjusted annually by the Consumer Price Index (CPI). In Scenario B, a lease requires base payments of $100,000 plus $0.50 per machine-hour of usage. Analyze why ASC 842 includes CPI-linked variable payments in the lease liability measurement but expenses usage-based variable payments as incurred, and compare the financial statement effects.',
  choices = '["ASC 842 treats CPI-linked payments and usage-based payments identically — both are included in the lease liability because they represent the total expected cost of the lease", "Usage-based payments are included in the liability while CPI-linked payments are excluded, because usage creates a more certain obligation than inflation", "Neither type is included in the lease liability — all variable payments are expensed as incurred under ASC 842 regardless of their basis", "CPI-linked payments are included in the initial lease liability measurement (using the index rate at commencement) because they are economically similar to fixed payments — the lessee knows the payment structure and cannot avoid the CPI adjustment, making the obligation measurable; usage-based payments are expensed as incurred because they depend on the lessee''s future operating decisions and are inherently unpredictable at commencement — including them would require forecasting activity levels that the lessee controls, undermining measurement reliability"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): Under ASC 842-10-30-5, variable lease payments based on an index or rate (like CPI) are included in the initial lease liability using the index value at lease commencement. The rationale is that these payments are functionally fixed — the lessee cannot avoid the obligation, and the payment amount is determinable at inception using the current index. When the index changes, the lessee remeasures the liability. Usage-based payments (per-mile, per-hour, per-unit) are excluded from the liability under ASC 842-10-55-225 because they depend on the lessee''s future operating decisions — the lessee can choose to use the asset more or less, making the obligation inherently variable and outside the scope of reliable initial measurement. On the income statement, CPI-linked payments affect both amortization and interest (through the ROU asset and liability), while usage-based payments appear as variable lease expense when incurred. (A) is wrong because ASC 842 explicitly distinguishes these two types. (B) reverses the treatment. (C) is wrong because index-linked payments are specifically included in the liability.',
  correct_index = 3
WHERE id = 3275;

-- ID 3280: Leases — Operating vs finance lease expense pattern comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Mercer Industries enters a 5-year lease for equipment with a right-of-use asset valued at $500,000 and no residual value guarantee. The lease is classified as an operating lease. If it were instead classified as a finance lease, the expense pattern would differ significantly. Compare the expense recognition patterns under each classification over the 5-year term, and analyze why total expense is identical but the timing pattern differs and which classification front-loads expense.',
  choices = '["Operating leases produce higher total expense than finance leases because operating leases include both depreciation and interest while finance leases only include depreciation", "Finance leases produce straight-line expense while operating leases produce front-loaded expense, because operating lease amortization uses an accelerated method", "Under both classifications, total lease expense over 5 years is identical (total payments plus any initial direct costs) — however, an operating lease recognizes a single straight-line lease expense each period, while a finance lease separately recognizes amortization (straight-line on the ROU asset) plus interest expense (declining over time as the liability decreases), producing a front-loaded pattern where Year 1 expense exceeds Year 5 expense; the finance lease front-loads because interest is calculated on the declining balance, creating higher combined expense in early years and lower in later years", "The expense patterns are identical under both classifications because ASC 842 eliminated the distinction between operating and finance lease expense recognition"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under ASC 842, total lease expense is the same regardless of classification — the lessee pays the same total consideration. The difference is timing. An operating lease recognizes a single straight-line lease expense each period, combining the effective amortization and interest into one level amount. A finance lease separates the two components: amortization of the ROU asset (typically straight-line, producing equal annual charges) and interest on the lease liability (calculated on the declining balance, producing higher charges in early periods and lower charges in later periods). The combined finance lease expense is therefore front-loaded — Year 1 has the highest total expense (amortization + maximum interest) while Year 5 has the lowest (same amortization + minimal interest). For a $500,000 asset, the front-loading can be significant enough to affect reported earnings materially in early periods. (A) is wrong because both classifications produce equal total expense over the lease term. (B) reverses the front-loading pattern. (D) is wrong because ASC 842 maintained the distinction in expense recognition patterns, which was a deliberate design choice.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 3280;

COMMIT;
